Depend on the generated cc_library to include the header: cc_library( name = "mylib", srcs = ["mylib.c"], deps = ["@my_kconfig//:config"],
) #include "config.h" #if CONFIG_FOO
/* FOO is enabled */
#endif The header follows the Kconfig tools' autoconf.h: bool and tristate symbols set to y define CONFIG_<name> as 1, a tristate set to m defines CONFIG_<name>_MODULE instead, int and hex values are unquoted, string values are quoted (an empty string is ""), and a symbol that is n is left undefined. So is a symbol the configuration gives no value because its depends on is unmet: its flag still exists, with the falsey default of its type, but config.h leaves it out so that #ifdef tells it apart from one set to 0 or "", as the kernel's headers expect. Some trees' sources expect a CONFIG_<name> for every symbol instead. write_invisible = True on kconfig.repo writes every symbol, as BusyBox's conf does: one the configuration gives no value goes to rendered.config, in its place, as # CONFIG_<name> is not set or with the empty value of its type (CONFIG_<name>="", CONFIG_<name>=0), and config.h defines it so.

11.body:
The generated repository includes a settings/ subpackage with config_setting targets for every flag. Bool flags produce two targets: CONFIG_<NAME>_Y (matching "true") and CONFIG_<NAME>_N (matching "false"). Int and string flags produce a target matching their Kconfig default value (named CONFIG_<NAME>_<value>; a character a target name may not hold, such as :, is _ in the name, while the value matched is the value itself). Use these directly in select(): cc_library( name = "mylib", srcs = ["mylib.c"], deps = select({ "@my_kconfig//settings:kconfig.CONFIG_FOO_Y": ["//extras:foo_support"], "//conditions:default": [], }),
) Custom values with kconfig_config_settings To match non-default values or multiple values for an int/string flag, load the generated settings.bzl macro and pass an options dict. The name parameter prefixes every generated target: load("@my_kconfig//:settings.bzl", "kconfig_config_settings") kconfig_config_settings( name = "settings", options = { "CONFIG_COUNT": ["1", "3", "5"], },
) This produces settings.CONFIG_FOO (bool, auto), settings.CONFIG_COUNT_1, settings.CONFIG_COUNT_3, and settings.CONFIG_COUNT_5. Flags not listed in options fall back to their Kconfig default. Flags with neither are simply skipped.

